diff --git a/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/README.md b/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/README.md
index 19298ba6..ae88ba0f 100644
--- a/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/README.md
+++ b/Code/CryPlugins/CinematicCamera/README.md
@@ -231,6 +231,7 @@ is deliberately inert (above).
 | Metering Mode | enum | Centre-weighted | **Scene Referred only.** Where in the frame the camera meters. **Average** reads the whole frame uniformly - the A/B reference, and outdoors the pattern that most often gets it wrong. **Centre-weighted** keeps full sensitivity in the middle and a quarter of it in the corners, which is what stops a bright sky from stopping the subject down. **Spot** reads a soft-edged disc a fifth of the frame height across (about 1.8 % of frame area), so you can put it on a face or a grey card and let everything else fall where it falls. |
 | Metering Band Low | 0-100 % | 50 | **Scene Referred only.** Discard everything below this percentile of metering weight before averaging. The camera meters a 64-bin histogram, not an average, and this is the bottom of the band it keeps. 50 throws away the darker half of the frame - shadow detail nobody meters for, and exactly what dragged an outdoor scene below mid grey. 0 keeps everything. |
 | Metering Band High | 0-100 % | 90 | **Scene Referred only.** Discard everything above this percentile. 90 throws away the top decile - sky, glints, the sun itself - so panning past the sun is a non-event instead of a stop-down. 100 keeps everything. The camera exposes so that the log-mean of what is **left** between the two lands on mid grey. Deliberately not Unreal's 10/90, which exists to make its histogram agree with its mean; the mean is the thing this replaces. |
