 | Sensor Clip (stops over grey) | 4-12 | 8.0 | **Scene Referred only.** The stops over mid grey at which the sensor saturates. Highlights are clipped per channel at this level, **after** the white balance, so a clipped highlight stays white at every colour temperature instead of taking the balance's tint - which is what every RAW converter does and why a blown sky in a real camera is white and not blue. Real cinema sensors hold **6-8 stops over grey** (ARRI ALEV III about 7.4, Sony Venice about 7.5; the advertised "14+ stops" is the sensor's TOTAL range, most of it *below* grey). Above about **10** the display transform has already turned everything white on its own - the ODT LUT's ACEScct domain ends at +10.3 stops - so higher values change only the EXR negative, never the picture. Lower = a smaller, earlier-clipping sensor (more flat white sky); higher = highlight colour and structure further up, until the display transform runs out. The lens effects upstream - bloom, glare, diffraction, depth of field - are deliberately **not** clipped: the lens sees the real light, so a sun a hundred thousand times over the clip still throws a flare that says so. |
